§ `Tests of the seller's seat`

§§ `Section 47: the appreciation, the inflationary amount and the real appreciation`

-- 3,000,000 less 2,000,000 acquisition, indexes 120 over 100, no expenses: remaining acquisition value 2,000,000; appreciation 1,000,000; adjusted 2,000,000 x 1.2 = 2,400,000; inflationary min(1,000,000, 400,000) = 400,000; real 600,000.
`sale one` MEANS `a sale for appreciation` (Date 1 3 2025) (Date 1 1 2015) 3_000_000 2_000_000 (`no expenses`) 0 120 100
#ASSERT `the remaining acquisition value` (`sale one`) EQUALS 2_000_000
#ASSERT `the appreciation` (`sale one`) EQUALS 1_000_000
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the inflationary amount` (`sale one`)) 400_000
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the real appreciation` (`sale one`)) 600_000
#ASSERT `the loss on the sale` (`sale one`) EQUALS 0

-- An improvement of 100,000 paid at index 120 (the divisor): remaining 2,100,000; appreciation 900,000; adjusted 2,400,000 + 100,000 x 120/120 = 2,500,000; inflationary 2,500,000 - 2,100,000 = 400,000; real 500,000.
`sale two` MEANS `a sale for appreciation` (Date 1 3 2025) (Date 1 1 2015) 3_000_000 2_000_000 ((LIST (`an expense of the kind` `an improvement or other deductible expense paid before the sale` 100_000 120))) 0 120 100
#ASSERT `the remaining acquisition value` (`sale two`) EQUALS 2_100_000
#ASSERT `the appreciation` (`sale two`) EQUALS 900_000
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the inflationary amount` (`sale two`)) 400_000
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the real appreciation` (`sale two`)) 500_000
#ASSERT `the loss on the sale` (`sale two`) EQUALS 0

-- The same improvement with divisor 100: adjusted 2,400,000 + 100,000 x 120/100 = 2,520,000; inflationary 420,000; real 480,000.
`sale three` MEANS `a sale for appreciation` (Date 1 3 2025) (Date 1 1 2015) 3_000_000 2_000_000 ((LIST (`an expense of the kind` `an improvement or other deductible expense paid before the sale` 100_000 100))) 0 120 100
#ASSERT `the remaining acquisition value` (`sale three`) EQUALS 2_100_000
#ASSERT `the appreciation` (`sale three`) EQUALS 900_000
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the inflationary amount` (`sale three`)) 420_000
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the real appreciation` (`sale three`)) 480_000
#ASSERT `the loss on the sale` (`sale three`) EQUALS 0

-- Consideration 2,300,000: appreciation 300,000, but the adjusted value exceeds the remaining value by 400,000. Fork F1: the inflationary amount is the PART OF THE APPRECIATION equal to that excess, so at most 300,000; real appreciation 0. (The rejected reading would give a real appreciation of -100,000.)
`sale four` MEANS `a sale for appreciation` (Date 1 3 2025) (Date 1 1 2015) 2_300_000 2_000_000 (`no expenses`) 0 120 100
#ASSERT `the remaining acquisition value` (`sale four`) EQUALS 2_000_000
#ASSERT `the appreciation` (`sale four`) EQUALS 300_000
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the inflationary amount` (`sale four`)) 300_000
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the real appreciation` (`sale four`)) 0
#ASSERT `the loss on the sale` (`sale four`) EQUALS 0

-- Consideration 1,900,000 against 2,000,000: no appreciation, a loss of 100,000, and no real appreciation.
`sale five` MEANS `a sale for appreciation` (Date 1 3 2025) (Date 1 1 2015) 1_900_000 2_000_000 (`no expenses`) 0 120 100
#ASSERT `the remaining acquisition value` (`sale five`) EQUALS 2_000_000
#ASSERT `the appreciation` (`sale five`) EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the inflationary amount` (`sale five`)) 0
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the real appreciation` (`sale five`)) 0
#ASSERT `the loss on the sale` (`sale five`) EQUALS 100_000

-- Depreciation 200,000: remaining 2,000,000 - 200,000 = 1,800,000; appreciation 1,200,000; adjusted (2,000,000 - 200,000) x 1.2 = 2,160,000; inflationary min(1,200,000, 360,000) = 360,000; real 840,000.
`sale six` MEANS `a sale for appreciation` (Date 1 3 2025) (Date 1 1 2015) 3_000_000 2_000_000 (`no expenses`) 200_000 120 100
#ASSERT `the remaining acquisition value` (`sale six`) EQUALS 1_800_000
#ASSERT `the appreciation` (`sale six`) EQUALS 1_200_000
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the inflationary amount` (`sale six`)) 360_000
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the real appreciation` (`sale six`)) 840_000
#ASSERT `the loss on the sale` (`sale six`) EQUALS 0

-- An expense of 50,000 paid after the sale at index 125 (sale index 120): its value is 50,000 x 120/125 = 48,000; remaining 2,048,000; appreciation 952,000; adjusted 2,400,000 + 48,000 = 2,448,000; inflationary 400,000; real 552,000.
`sale seven` MEANS `a sale for appreciation` (Date 1 3 2025) (Date 1 1 2015) 3_000_000 2_000_000 ((LIST (`an expense of the kind` `an expense paid after the sale` 50_000 125))) 0 120 100
#ASSERT `the remaining acquisition value` (`sale seven`) EQUALS 2_048_000
#ASSERT `the appreciation` (`sale seven`) EQUALS 952_000
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the inflationary amount` (`sale seven`)) 400_000
#ASSERT `the two numbers agree to a cent` (`the real appreciation` (`sale seven`)) 552_000
#ASSERT `the loss on the sale` (`sale seven`) EQUALS 0

-- Property tax of 10,000 paid before 1 April 1972, divisor 100: the remaining value takes all 10,000 (2,010,000); the adjusted value takes HALF (5,000) x 120/100 = 6,000, so 2,406,000; appreciation 990,000; inflationary 396,000; real 594,000.
`sale eight` MEANS `a sale for appreciation` (Date 1 3 2025) (Date 1 1 2015) 3_000_000 2_000_000 ((LIST (`an expense of the kind` `property tax paid up to 31 March 1972` 10_000 100))) 0 120 100
